<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tao</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dao, 道</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tao or Dao (道) is, in East Asian philosophy and religions, the source and fundamental principle of the universe. The Chinese character means way, path or road, and philosophically doctrine or principle. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a>, Tao is self-existent, formless, eternal and the source of all existence, and it is said to be beyond description; the text distinguishes the named Tao from the unnamed Tao. It describes Tao as feminine, a mother of all things, and Taoist scriptures treat the Ultra Supreme Elder Lord as an embodiment of it. Scholars distinguish its ethical use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> from its more metaphysical use in philosophical Taoism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tao.jpg" alt="Tao" width="330" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cangjie6 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tao_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a> describes something undifferentiated and yet complete that existed before heaven and earth, soundless, formless and dependent on nothing. In chapter 25 it says of it: &quot;I do not know its name; I call it Tao.&quot; The book has two parts: the Tao Ching, chapters 1 to 37, and the Te Ching, chapters 38 to 81. Tao is often described as feminine, a mother who gives birth to all existence, and following it is called &quot;keeping to the Mother&quot;. Some ancient Chinese classics describe a supreme goddess, Holy Mother the Original Lord, as an embodiment of Tao, who is said to have taught the Yellow Emperor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Lao Tzu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>De, meaning power, virtue or integrity, refers to proper adherence to Tao, the cultivation of the way. Taoists considered social rules at best a derivative reflection of natural interaction and at worst a calcified structure that created conflict, which led to disputes with Confucians. Confucius presents a humanistic Tao and only rarely speaks of the Way of Heaven, a contrast the early Confucian Xunzi noted. In the Tang dynasty Han Yu paired Tao and De and framed a &quot;tradition of the Tao&quot; to reject <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. During the Song dynasty Shao Yong saw the Tao as the origin of heaven and earth, and Zhang Zai as a vital force bound up with qi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Buddhist monks translating texts into Chinese used Chinese concepts close to Buddhist ideas, such as the Tao, which helped the religion spread. In some Chinese translations the logos of John 1:1 is rendered as 道. The character is explained in the Shuowen Jiezi dictionary of 121 CE as combining the radical for &quot;go&quot; with 首, &quot;head&quot;, signifying a head going or leading the way. Its earliest forms appear on bronzes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. The English word is recorded from 1736 as Tao, and many Western languages changed to the spelling Dao after the International Organization for Standardization adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanyu-pinyin">Pinyin</a> in 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
